.TH IPPFIX 8 "August 2026" "1.0" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
ippfix \- IPP proxy that repairs print jobs for printers with a limited font
cache.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ippfix
[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-advertise\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-advertise\-hostname\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-also\-advertise\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-alert\-mail\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-alert\-max\-attachment\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-\-alert\-max\-per\-hour\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-alert\-spool\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-alert\-timeout\fR \fISEC\fR]
[\fB\-\-archive\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-list\fR [\fIURL\fR]]
[\fB\-\-max\-connections\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-idle\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-require\-tls\fR]
[\fB\-\-convert\-threshold\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-pdf\-bytes\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\-formats\fR]
[\fB\-\-fail\-closed\fR]
[\fB\-\-archive\-max\-bytes\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-ipv6\fR]
[\fB\-\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-converter\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-advertise\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-convert\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
\fI[NAME=]URI\fR...

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ippfix
accepts print jobs over IPP, rewrites each PDF so that it contains no embedded
font programs, and forwards it to a real printer. Everything else is relayed
untouched.
.PP
It exists because HP LaserJet Pro printers run a combined PostScript/PDF
interpreter with a fixed per\-page budget for embedded fonts and the glyphs
drawn from them. Exceeding that budget aborts the interpreter: the printer
warms up, reports the job
.BR completed ,
and marks nothing. Sometimes the first few pages emerge and the job then stops.
No error reaches the client and none is shown on the panel. Where the device
records it at all, its event log reports an assertion failure in
.BR fontcache.c .
.PP
The budget covers both the glyphs drawn and the embedded font programs they
come from, and the two trade against each other, but no rule relating the two
has survived testing. What is established is that the font's hinting is
causally involved \(em the same 900\-glyph page fails with a hinted font and
prints with the hinting bytecode removed \(em and that it is a property of the
font rather than of the application, which is why printing web pages has been
more reliable than printing PDFs. Four cost models were fitted to measured
outcomes and every one was falsified; see
.B CALIBRATION
below.
.PP
The defect appears in firmware builds years apart and is unlikely to be fixed.
Client\-side changes affect how often it is reached: Chrome 130 through 144
embedded a separate font program for every
.I strike
of a typeface, including one per text colour, which multiplied the cost of an
ordinary page. Chrome 145 removed the colour component and normalises text size
out of the key, so current versions embed far fewer font programs than that era
did.
.PP
.B ippfix
converts glyphs to filled paths using Ghostscript's
.B \-dNoOutputFonts
option. No font program reaches the printer, which makes the failure
structurally impossible rather than merely less likely. Text remains vector, so
the printer's own rasteriser still renders it at full device precision and its
edge enhancement still applies. Rasterising the page instead would commit its
geometry to the device grid before the printer saw it, forfeit the printer's
halftoning, and inflate a small job into tens of megabytes.
.PP
This addresses one fault. Two others have been reproduced on the same device
and are not fixed here: a page of vector colour glyphs is rejected with
.BR document\-format\-error ,
and a soft mask whose
.B /BC
backdrop array does not match the component count of its group's colour space
is accepted, reported complete, and marked nowhere. Both are properties of the
document that conversion preserves faithfully. The distributed
.I DIAGNOSING.md
records how to tell them apart.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I [NAME=]URI
A printer to proxy, given as an
.B ipp://
or
.B ipps://
URI. The optional
.I NAME
becomes the queue name, published at
.BI /ipp/ NAME\fR.
Without one, the queue is named
.BR print .
May be repeated to serve several printers from one daemon.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-advertise " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Address that clients should use to reach this server. Autodetected by default,
which is wrong on hosts with several addresses.
.TP
.B \-\-advertise\-hostname " \fINAME\fR"
Host name to publish in the DNS\-SD SRV record. Clients build the URI they
remember out of this name, so it is used every time they print, not only while
they are discovering the printer.
.IP
The default is the
.B \-\-advertise
address itself rather than a
.I .local
name. A
.I .local
name has to be resolved by multicast DNS on every print, and multicast does not
cross a VPN, a routed subnet, or a wireless network with client isolation: the
printer is found once and then quietly stops working from anywhere else. An
address literal needs no resolution at all. The cost is that the address becomes
part of what clients remember, so it should be reserved or static \- which is
already true of
.BR \-\-advertise ,
since every URI this proxy hands out is built from it.
.IP
Give a name to publish one instead, or
.B auto
for this system's
.I .local
name. An IPv6 literal is never used by default: clients paste the name straight
into
.IR ipp://HOST:PORT/... ,
where a bare IPv6 address needs square brackets they do not add, so a v6\-only
.B \-\-advertise
falls back to the system name. AAAA records are published either way; this
setting only decides which name clients are handed.
.TP
.B \-\-also\-advertise " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Additional address to publish in the DNS\-SD records; may be repeated. By
default the stable, globally scoped IPv6 addresses of the same interface as
.B \-\-advertise
are published alongside it, so dual\-stack clients are offered IPv6 without
extra configuration.
.IP
Only that interface's addresses are used. On a multi\-homed host, publishing
every address the machine happens to have invites clients to try one they
cannot route to, which appears as a long stall rather than a clear failure.
Privacy, deprecated and tentative addresses are excluded because they rotate or
are not valid for new connections, and link\-local addresses are excluded
because they need a scope identifier a DNS\-SD record cannot carry.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ipv6
Publish only the IPv4 address, for networks where IPv6 exists but is not
routable.
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-mail " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Send mail to
.I ADDRESS
when a job does not print. Off unless set.
.IP
The failures this proxy exists for are silent: the printer accepts the job,
reports it completed, and marks nothing, so every layer above it repeats that
success and nobody finds out. When this is set, each print job is followed to
its terminal state and judged on what the printer says it marked rather than on
whether the request succeeded. IPP reports this itself \(em a job that completes
having marked nothing gives
.I job\-impressions\-completed
of zero \(em so no other protocol is needed.
.IP
The report names the queue, the job, what conversion did, the sequence of states
the printer went through, and the document's structure: producer, embedded font
programs, shading and pattern types, transparency groups, soft masks and a
digest. It contains no text and no images from the document; the aim is to make
a fault reproducible, not to copy what somebody printed.
.IP
This is off by default because a printer that does not report impressions
honestly would report every job as lost. Delivery uses
.IR /usr/sbin/sendmail ,
so a local mail transport agent is required; if it is missing or refuses, the
report is written to the log instead of being lost. Following a job happens
after the client has been answered, so it never delays one.
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-max\-per\-hour " \fIN\fR"
Send at most
.I N
alerts an hour, default 6. Suppressed ones are logged, and the count is carried
into the next message, so a flood is reported as a flood instead of becoming
one.
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-spool " \fIDIR\fR"
Write alerts into
.I DIR
for a helper to deliver, rather than running
.BR sendmail (1)
directly. Default
.IR /var/lib/ippfix/alerts .
.IP
Delivery needs membership of the mail group, which on a typical system also
grants read access to the mail system's credential file. The daemon is the most
exposed component here and is not given either: it writes a complete message to
this directory, and
.I ippfix\-alert.path
notices and hands it to
.BR sendmail (1)
from a unit that never sees a document or a network connection. This is the same
separation used for document conversion.
.IP
Set to the empty string to deliver directly, which is appropriate when running
by hand or outside the supplied units.
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-timeout " \fISEC\fR"
Give up following a job after
.I SEC
seconds, default 600. A job still unfinished then is reported as such.
.TP
.B \-\-alert\-max\-attachment " \fIMB\fR"
Attach at most
.I MB
megabytes of documents to a report, default 8.
.IP
A report carries the two documents that matter, and they are not the same one:
the job as the client sent it, which requires
.BR \-\-archive ,
and the job as this proxy handed it to the printer. A fault that survives
conversion is a different bug from one conversion introduced, and only having
both tells them apart. Attachments are sent as they are so they can be fed
straight to the tools this page names; a document that would not otherwise fit
is compressed, and one that still does not fit is named but not attached.
.I 0
attaches nothing, which leaves the report itself \- states, timings and
structure \- intact.
.IP
Note what this means: a report can carry a document somebody printed. That is
the point of it, but it is also mail. Send it somewhere that reflects how
sensitive the printing is.
.TP
.B \-\-archive " \fIDIR\fR"
Diagnostic only. Keep a copy of every job exactly as it arrived, before
conversion, together with a short text file recording the queue, job name,
document format and what conversion did.
.IP
This writes the documents users print to disk. It is off by default. The
directory is created mode 0700 and the files 0600, owned by the service
account, but anyone who can read them can read everything printed while it was
enabled. It exists because the failure this proxy works around is silent and
depends on document content, so without the document that provoked it there is
almost nothing to go on. Enable it while chasing a specific problem, and turn
it off again afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-archive\-max " \fIN\fR"
Keep at most
.I N
archived jobs, deleting the oldest first. Default 50. Bounds the disk cost of a
flag left on by accident.
.TP
.B \-\-cert " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS certificate. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt .
.TP
.B \-\-converter " \fIPATH\fR"
Helper that rewrites a PDF read on standard input and writes the result to
standard output. Defaults to
.I defont
alongside the program, so it is correct whichever way this was installed.
.TP
.B \-\-key " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS private key. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.key .
.TP
.B \-\-list " [\fIURL\fR]"
Print the queues a running instance serves and exit, then stop. Intended for
configuring clients by address rather than by discovery: mDNS is not available
everywhere, and some sites prefer printers pinned by address so that discovery
cannot silently point users somewhere else. Defaults to the instance on this
host. The same listing is served as JSON at
.I /queues.json
and as a table at the daemon's HTTP root.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-connections " \fIN\fR"
Refuse connections beyond
.I N
concurrent ones. Default 64. Without a bound, connections that open and then
say nothing accumulate until the service hits its task limit, after which it
can accept nothing further until restarted.
.TP
.B \-\-idle\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Drop a connection that stops speaking for this long. Default 30. Applies to
every stage of a request, so a client cannot hold a thread by sending a partial
header and waiting.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-tls
Refuse plaintext IPP and accept only implicit TLS. Off by default, because
clients that discover a printer over DNS-SD commonly choose plaintext and would
otherwise simply fail to print.
.TP
.B \-\-convert\-threshold " \fIN\fR"
Leave a job untouched when its estimated font cost is at or below
.IR N .
Default 0, which converts every PDF: the test is skipped entirely rather than
compared against zero.
.IP
Outlining is not free \(em it replaces every drawn glyph with an inline path,
which costs about a third of a second and roughly doubles the size of a real
job \(em so skipping it for documents that certainly do not need it would be
worth having. The obstacle is that no estimate has been shown to predict the
printer's behaviour; see
.B CALIBRATION
below. Set this only if you have measured your own workload, and treat it as an
optimisation rather than as a safety margin. A file whose cost cannot be
determined is always converted, never assumed cheap.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-pdf\-bytes " \fIMB\fR"
Rasterise rather than send an outlined PDF larger than this. Default 60.
Overridden by the printer's own
.B pdf\-k\-octets\-supported
where it reports one, so the shipped default only applies to a device that
declares no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-formats
Offer clients every document format the printer supports, including
PostScript.
.IP
PostScript is withheld by default. It is interpreted by exactly the task that
fails on these devices, and it cannot be converted the way PDF is: handing
PostScript to Ghostscript means running its PostScript interpreter, which this
program is careful never to do. Offering it would advertise a repaired queue
that silently is not one. PCL and PCL\-XL are offered either way \(em they are
a separate interpreter on the device, and are relayed unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-closed
Reject a PDF that cannot be converted, instead of forwarding it unchanged.
By default a conversion failure relays the original, on the grounds that a job
which might not print beats one that prints wrongly. That is the safe choice
for printing and the unsafe one for the guarantee this proxy exists to give,
because whoever sends the document also decides whether conversion fails. This
option inverts that trade.
.TP
.B \-\-archive\-max\-bytes " \fIMB\fR"
Total size cap for the archive, in megabytes. Default 512. A count alone is not
a bound when the documents are chosen by whoever is printing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-advertise
Do not publish the queues over DNS\-SD. Useful when discovery is handled
elsewhere.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-convert
Relay jobs untouched. Intended for comparison against a converting instance,
to confirm that conversion is what makes the difference.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
Port to listen on. Default 631. Under the supplied units systemd binds that
port and passes the descriptor, so no capability is needed; started by hand it
requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or an adjusted
.IR net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start .
.TP
.B \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Time allowed for each conversion and each request to the printer. Default 300.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log protocol detail.

.SH CONVERSION
A job passes through up to three stages, stopping at the first that suffices.
.TP
.B Relayed untouched
The document is not a PDF, conversion failed, the converted form still
contained a font program, or the conversion lost a whole class of drawing
construct and was therefore discarded. A non\-zero
.B \-\-convert\-threshold
also lands a cheap job here, but that is off by default.
.TP
.B Text outlined
Glyphs become filled paths, so no font program reaches the printer. Text stays
vector, so the printer's own rasteriser still renders it at full device
precision.
.TP
.B Rasterised
Only when the outlined form would exceed what the printer will accept as a PDF.
These devices advertise
.B pdf\-k\-octets\-supported
of 0\-75000, and outlining inlines an outline at every glyph occurrence, so a
long document can pass that. The page is then sent as 600 dpi contone URF.
Fidelity is lower, because the geometry is committed to the device grid before
the printer sees it, but the printer still applies its own halftoning and edge
enhancement, and the job prints rather than being rejected. The job's document
format is updated to match.
.PP
Which raster format, colour space, resolution and PDF size limit apply is read
from the printer's own attributes and travels with each document, because the
converter has no network and cannot ask. The environment variables
.BR MAX_PDF_BYTES " (default 60000000),"
.BR RASTER_DPI " (600) and"
.B RASTER_COLORSPACE
(19 for sRGB; 18 for 8\-bit grey, which halves the size and avoids
composite\-black fringing on text) supply the defaults used when
.B defont
is run by hand, without that header.
.PP
The raster tier is chosen from the size of the converted document before the
job is sent. Nothing reacts to what the printer does with a job afterwards, so
a document the printer rejects, or accepts and does not print, is not retried
in another form.

.SH CALIBRATION
.B By default every PDF is converted, because predicting which ones need it
.B did not work.
Four models were fitted to measured outcomes and each was falsified: the glyph
count a font declares \(em a font declaring 65535 glyphs while drawing 27
printed perfectly \(em the glyphs a page draws \(em 1264 printed where 700
failed \(em the size of the embedded font program, and the outline complexity
of the glyphs actually used, where 519 glyphs in 47 kB failed and 519 glyphs in
50 kB printed. Whatever the firmware counts is not visible in the document.
.PP
Converting unconditionally costs roughly a third of a second and about double
the file size on a real job, which is a better trade than a prediction that has
been wrong repeatedly. A cost estimate is still computed and written to the log
for diagnosis.
.PP
.B \-\-convert\-threshold
can skip conversion for jobs scoring at or below a given value, for a site that
has measured its own workload and wants the optimisation. It is off by default
and nothing depends on the estimate being accurate.
.PP
To investigate a printer directly, note that a job which exceeds its limit
marks no paper, so only the passing probes cost anything. The device's own page
counter is the reliable signal, because an affected printer reports the job
completed either way. The Printer MIB (RFC 3805) exposes it on any network
printer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
snmpget \-v2c \-c public PRINTER 1.3.6.1.2.1.43.10.2.1.4.1.1
.fi
.RE

.SH BEHAVIOUR
Both plaintext IPP and implicit TLS are accepted on the same port, and both
IPv4 and IPv6 are served.
.PP
Printer attributes are mirrored from the real device on every request. Only
identity and URIs are rewritten \(em the queue name, its UUID, the URIs clients
should use, and the locations of the printer's icons and localised strings,
which are re\-served by this daemon because clients may have no route to the
printer's own web server. Capabilities and status pass through unchanged, so
duplex, trays, media, colour modes and live supply and error state are always
correct without configuration.
.PP
Documents that are not PDF are relayed untouched, as are PDFs whose conversion
fails or leaves any font program behind. A job that might not print is
preferable to one that prints something wrong.
.PP
Jobs are forwarded one at a time, because affected printers report
.B multiple\-document\-jobs\-supported
as false and mishandle a second job arriving mid\-transfer.

.SH FILES
Two layouts are supported. The packages install under
.IR /usr/lib/ippfix ,
with the command line in a conffile;
.B install.sh
installs under
.I /usr/local/lib/ippfix
instead, with the command line in the unit.
.TP
.I /etc/ippfix/ippfix.conf
Packaged installations only. Read by the unit as an environment file;
.B IPPFIX_ARGS
holds the whole command line. The units are conditional on this file existing,
so nothing runs and no port is taken until it does. An example is installed as
.IR /usr/share/doc/ippfix/ippfix.conf.example .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/ippfix.service
.B install.sh
installations only: the service unit, symlinked from the installation
directory. Edit
.B ExecStart
to configure the printers.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/ippfix/defont
PDF conversion helper, run as its own confined service. May be run by hand for
diagnosis:
.IP
.EX
defont \-\-selfcheck
.EE
.IP
reports whether the installed Ghostscript preserves function\-based shadings,
which is how browsers emit conic and repeating CSS gradients. Releases before
10.05 discard them; a conversion that loses one is thrown away in favour of the
original, so such a document keeps its appearance and forgoes only the font fix.
See
.IR DIAGNOSING.md .
.TP
.I /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt\fR, \fI/etc/ippfix/ippfix.key
Self\-signed TLS credentials, generated on first start if absent.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ippfix/archive
Default location for
.BR \-\-archive .
Aged out after seven days by a
.I tmpfiles.d
rule.
.TP
.I /usr/share/ippfix/scripts/
Diagnostic tools, packaged installations only:
.IR probe\-printer.py ,
.IR make\-reproducer.py ,
.IR make\-html\-reproducer.py ,
.I check\-softmask.py
and
.IR fidelity\-check.py .

.SH EXAMPLES
Serve one printer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve two, under explicit names:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix upstairs=ipp://printer1.example/ipp/print \\
       downstairs=ipp://printer2.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a second, non\-converting instance for comparison:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix \-\-port 6310 \-\-no\-advertise \-\-no\-convert \\
       ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each job is logged with its queue, operation, and the result of conversion:
.PP
.RS
.nf
upstairs   Print\-Job   HTTP 200  [outlined 393836 \-> 76789 bytes in 0.2s (font cost 465)]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B relayed
indicates the job was passed through unchanged, with the reason in brackets.
The font cost is recorded for diagnosis; nothing is decided by it unless
.B \-\-convert\-threshold
is set.
.PP
Because an affected printer reports success whether or not it printed, the
reliable check is the device's own total impression count, which does not
advance for a job that died.
.B \-\-alert\-mail
performs that check on every job by following it to its terminal state. To do
it by hand, read the RFC 3805 counter over SNMP before and after; on HP devices
that counter and the interpreter's assertion log are also readable over HTTP
from
.I /DevMgmt/ProductUsageDyn.xml
and
.IR /DevMgmt/ProductLogsDyn.xml ,
which are vendor paths this program neither uses nor depends on.

.SH NOTES
If clients can also reach the printers directly, they will see both those and
the proxy queues, and users will choose arbitrarily between them. Suppress the
printers' own
.BR _ipp ,
.B _ipps
and
.B _pdl\-datastream
advertisements, but leave
.B _uscan
and
.B _uscans
alone or scanning will stop working.
.PP
Only one mDNS responder may hold UDP port 5353. With
.B systemd\-resolved
present, set
.B MulticastDNS=no
in
.I /etc/systemd/resolved.conf
and restart it, or run with
.BR \-\-no\-advertise .

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gs (1),
.BR cups (1),
.BR snmpget (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
The distributed documentation:
.I README.md
for what the fault looks like and how to install,
.I DEPLOYMENT.md
for making clients use the proxy,
.I DIAGNOSING.md
for the three known firmware faults and how to reproduce them,
.I INTERNALS.md
for the code, and
.I OPEN-QUESTIONS.md
for what is unsolved. Packaged installations keep these in
.IR /usr/share/doc/ippfix/ .

